course: dictionary
lesson: 8763
title: Several: more than two, not very many
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Several» tells you there is more than one, but not a lot. It means more than two, but not very many. «Several friends» means a small group of more than two friends, not one or two, and not a crowd.
explain: Cocoa farmers let the beans ferment «for several days» under leaves. Ferment means slowly change and turn a little sour. Several days is more than two days, but it does not say exactly how many, so you cannot count the days from the word several.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Most flushes use several litres» tells you a flush uses more than two litres, and probably not a huge amount. Always ask: could I swap in a few? If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: «Several» means more than two, but not very many.
fact: «Several» is not an exact number. Use it when you do not need to say the exact amount.
fact: As a pronoun, «several» means a few of a group: «Several of the cakes were eaten.»
fact: In maths, «several» does not tell you how many groups there are. The problem must give the number somewhere else.
fact: Everyday phrases include «several times» and «several days».
exercise: «Several of the cakes were eaten.» What does several mean here? | *A few of the cakes | Exactly ten cakes | All of the cakes | None of the cakes
exercise: «Most flushes use several litres.» What does several mean here? | Hundreds of litres | *More than two litres, but not a huge amount | Exactly one litre | Less than one litre
exercise: «When there are several equal groups, we multiply.» Why can't you find the exact number from the word several? | *Because several is not an exact number, so the problem must say how many elsewhere | Because several means the same as all | Because several always means ten | Because several is a number like five
test: «Picasso showed things from several sides at once.» Which word could replace several without changing the meaning much? | every | *a few | none | exactly one
test: What does «several days» mean? | *More than two days, but not very many | A hundred days exactly | Exactly one day | Less than one day
exam: Which is the best meaning of several? | *More than two, but not very many | All of them | Hardly any at all | Exactly two
